    34	
    35	## Definition
    36	
    37	Let $\gamma$ be an ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]). Since $\gamma$ is the set of
    38	ordinals below it and $\xi < \eta$ means $\xi \in \eta$, the following two
    39	families of subsets of $\gamma$ are defined for $\beta \in \gamma$ and
    40	$\alpha \in \gamma$:
    41	
    42	$$[0,\beta] \;:=\; \{\, \xi \in \gamma : \xi \le \beta \,\} \;=\; \beta^{+}, \qquad (\alpha,\beta] \;:=\; \{\, \xi \in \gamma : \alpha < \xi \le \beta \,\} \;=\; \beta^{+} \setminus \alpha^{+} .$$
    43	
    44	Both identifications are immediate: $\beta^{+} = \beta \cup \{\beta\}$ is the set
    45	of ordinals $\le \beta$, and it is a subset of $\gamma$ because $\gamma$ is
    46	transitive and $\beta \in \gamma$ ([[def-ordinal]], [[lem-ordinal-basics]]).
    47	
    48	Put
    49	
    50	$$\mathcal{B}_\gamma \;:=\; \{\, [0,\beta] : \beta \in \gamma \,\} \;\cup\; \{\, (\alpha,\beta] : \alpha, \beta \in \gamma,\ \alpha < \beta \,\} .$$
    51	
    52	**$\mathcal{B}_\gamma$ is a basis for a unique topology on $\gamma$**
    53	([[thm-basis-criterion]], [[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]), and that topology is
    54	the **order topology on $\gamma$**. The obligation is discharged here.
    55	
    56	*(B1), covering.* If $\xi \in \gamma$ then $\xi \in [0,\xi] \in \mathcal{B}_\gamma$,
    57	so $\bigcup \mathcal{B}_\gamma = \gamma$. For $\gamma = 0 = \varnothing$ the
    58	family is empty and $\bigcup \varnothing = \varnothing = \gamma$, so (B1) holds
    59	there too.
    60	
    61	*(B2), intersections.* By trichotomy of the ordinals ([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]])
    62	any two ordinals have a maximum and a minimum, namely the larger and the smaller
    63	of the two, and for $\alpha_1, \alpha_2, \beta_1, \beta_2 \in \gamma$:
    64	
    65	- $[0,\beta_1] \cap [0,\beta_2] = [0, \min\{\beta_1,\beta_2\}]$;
    66	- $[0,\beta_1] \cap (\alpha_2,\beta_2] = (\alpha_2, \min\{\beta_1,\beta_2\}]$ when
    67	  $\alpha_2 < \min\{\beta_1,\beta_2\}$, and $\varnothing$ otherwise;
    68	- $(\alpha_1,\beta_1] \cap (\alpha_2,\beta_2] = (\max\{\alpha_1,\alpha_2\},\ \min\{\beta_1,\beta_2\}]$
    69	  when $\max\{\alpha_1,\alpha_2\} < \min\{\beta_1,\beta_2\}$, and $\varnothing$
    70	  otherwise.
    71	
    72	In each case the intersection is either a member of $\mathcal{B}_\gamma$ or
    73	empty, and in the empty case (B2) is vacuous, having no point to test. So (B2)
    74	holds, and [[thm-basis-criterion]] supplies the topology and its uniqueness.
    75	
    76	**This definition is for ordinals only, and it says so.** The general order
    77	topology of a linearly ordered set takes the open intervals, together with the
    78	initial and final rays, as a basis. For an ordinal that family is the wrong one:
    79	a successor $\beta^{+}$ has an immediate predecessor, so the smallest open
    80	interval around it is already $\{\beta^{+}\}$, but no *interval* of the form
    81	$(\alpha,\eta)$ isolates $0$, and the initial segments must be supplied
    82	separately. The family $\mathcal{B}_\gamma$ above is exactly the general order
    83	basis for an ordinal, rewritten so that no case analysis is needed; nothing here
    84	claims to define the order topology of an arbitrary linearly ordered set, and no
    85	statement on this page is about such a set.
    86	
    87	**Isolated and non-isolated points.** Every ordinal is $0$, a successor, or a
    88	limit ([[def-limit-ordinal]]). If $\xi = 0$ then $\{\xi\} = [0,0]$ is basic open;
    89	if $\xi = \alpha^{+}$ then $\{\xi\} = (\alpha, \xi]$ is basic open; so every
    90	non-limit point of $\gamma$ is isolated. If $\xi$ is a limit ordinal then every
    91	basic set containing $\xi$ contains some $(\alpha,\xi]$ with $\alpha < \xi$, and
    92	$\alpha^{+} < \xi$ because $\xi$ is a limit, so $\alpha^{+}$ is a second point of
    93	that basic set; hence a limit point of $\gamma$ is not isolated. In particular
    94	$\omega$, the least limit ordinal ([[lem-omega-least-limit-ordinal]]), is the
    95	unique non-isolated point of $\omega + 1$, and every ordinal $\gamma \le \omega$
    96	carries the discrete topology ([[def-standard-topologies]]).
    97	
    98	## Remarks
    99	
   100	- **The basis members are clopen**, and that is proved as the next item; it is
   101	  the single fact that makes ordinal spaces easy to place in the separation
   102	  hierarchy, since a clopen basis gives regularity at once.
   103	
   104	- **$\gamma$ is a set of ordinals and also a space.** The notations $[0,\beta]$
   105	  and $(\alpha,\beta]$ are relative to the ambient $\gamma$: the same symbols in
   106	  a larger ordinal denote larger sets. Where two ordinals are in play the ambient
   107	  one is named.
   108	
   109	- **Nothing here needs any choice principle.** Every fact used above is a theorem
   110	  of ZF ([[lem-ordinal-basics]], [[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]]).
   111	
   112	- **This is the ordinal instance of a general construction.** The order topology
   113	  of an arbitrary linearly ordered set is now defined elsewhere in the library
   114	  ([[def-order-topology-on-a-linearly-ordered-set]]), by open intervals together
   115	  with the initial and final rays. $\mathcal{B}_\gamma$ above is not that
   116	  construction applied verbatim to $\gamma$ — the note two Remarks up already
   117	  explains why a raw open-interval basis is the wrong family for an ordinal —
   118	  but it generates the same topology: every basic open set of the general
   119	  construction is a union of members of $\mathcal{B}_\gamma$ and conversely,
   120	  since both bases are generated by the same order relation on the same
   121	  underlying set. So this definition is the ordinal special case of that one,
   122	  restated in a form that needs no case analysis, not a second, competing
   123	  notion.

