items/ex-the-oscillating-zigzag-curve-computed.md:81:[A5] $X$ is locally connected at $x$ when every open $U \ni x$ contains an open connected $V$ with $x \in V \subseteq U$; if $S$ is open in $X$ then a subset of $S$ is open in $S$ exactly when it is open in $X$ ([[def-locally-connected]], [[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
items/ex-the-oscillating-zigzag-curve-computed.md:95:3.1 Local connectedness holds at every point of $G$: let $p \in G$ and let $U$ be open in $\overline{G}$ with $p \in U$. Then $U \cap G$ is open in $\overline{G}$ by step 2.1, hence open in $G$ by [A5], $G$ being open; $G$ is locally connected by [L1], so there is $V$ open in $G$ and connected with $p \in V \subseteq U \cap G$; and $V$ is open in $\overline{G}$ by [A5]. [step 2.1, L1, A5]
items/ex-the-oscillating-zigzag-curve-computed.md:101:- **The two path components have different topological characters.** $\Sigma$ is homeomorphic to a closed bounded interval and $G$ to a half-open one, and only the first is closed in $\overline{G}$. So the partition into path components is not a partition into clopen pieces, which is exactly what [[thm-connected-and-locally-path-connected-implies-path-connected]] would supply if the space were locally path-connected — and it is not, by claim 4.
items/ex-the-oscillating-zigzag-curve-computed.md:103:- **Why $\overline{G}$ has one component and two path components at once.** Connectedness is destroyed only by a clopen splitting, and adjoining $\Sigma$ to $G$ creates none: every neighbourhood of a point of $\Sigma$ meets $G$ by [[thm-closure-characterisation-top]], claim 1, and claim 2 of [[lem-the-oscillating-zigzag-curve]]. Path-connectedness is destroyed by a single continuity failure along one map, and that is enough.
