    38	
    39	## Definition
    40	
    41	Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]), with
    42	subsets carrying the subspace topology ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]) and
    43	connectedness as in [[def-connected-space]]. Let $x \in X$.
    44	
    45	- The **connected component of $x$** is
    46	  $$C(x) \;:=\; \bigcup \{\, A \subseteq X : x \in A \text{ and } A \text{ is connected} \,\} .$$
    47	  A **component of $X$** is a set of the form $C(x)$ for some $x \in X$.
    48	- The **quasicomponent of $x$** is
    49	  $$Q(x) \;:=\; \bigcap \{\, K \subseteq X : x \in K \text{ and } K \text{ is clopen in } X \,\} .$$
    50	  A **quasicomponent of $X$** is a set of the form $Q(x)$.
    51	- $X$ is **totally disconnected** when $C(x) = \{x\}$ for every $x \in X$.
    52	
    53	**Both are well posed, and the obligations are discharged here.** The family
    54	united in the definition of $C(x)$ is nonempty, since the singleton $\{x\}$ is
    55	connected: a singleton admits no separation, a separation requiring two disjoint
    56	nonempty pieces. Every member of that family contains $x$, so
    57	[[thm-unions-of-connected-sets]] claim 1 applies and **$C(x)$ is connected**;
    58	being a union of every connected set through $x$, it contains each of them, so
    59	$C(x)$ is *the largest connected subset of $X$ containing $x$*. The family
    60	intersected in the definition of $Q(x)$ is nonempty as well, since $X$ itself is
    61	clopen ([[def-topological-space]]), so the intersection is a set; it contains
    62	$x$, every member doing so.
    63	
    64	**Both notions are defined by a property of $X$, not of an ambient space.** A
    65	component of a subspace $S \subseteq X$ means a component of the space $S$, and
    66	is written $C_S(y)$ when the space needs naming. The same holds for
    67	quasicomponents.
    68	
    69	**Totally disconnected, spelled out.** $X$ is totally disconnected exactly when
    70	every connected subset of $X$ has at most one point: if some connected $A$ had
    71	two points $x \ne y$ then $A \subseteq C(x)$ would give $C(x) \ne \{x\}$, and
    72	conversely if $C(x) \ne \{x\}$ then $C(x)$ is a connected set with at least two
    73	points. The empty space is totally disconnected, having no point to test.
    74	
    75	**A discrete space is totally disconnected.** Let $X$ carry the discrete topology
    76	([[def-standard-topologies]]) and let $A \subseteq X$ have two distinct points
    77	$x, y$. Every subset of $A$ is open in $A$, so $(\{x\}, A \setminus \{x\})$ is a
    78	pair of open, disjoint, nonempty sets covering $A$, that is a separation. Hence
    79	no connected subset has two points and every component is a singleton. The
    80	converse fails: total disconnectedness does not force the topology to be
    81	discrete.
    82	
    83	## Remarks
    84	
    85	- **Why two notions and not one.** The component of $x$ is built from the
    86	  *connected* sets through $x$ and the quasicomponent from the *clopen* sets
    87	  containing $x$. One is an inner approximation, assembled from below out of
    88	  pieces known to be connected; the other is an outer approximation, cut down
    89	  from above by every partition of $X$ into two clopen pieces. They always
    90	  satisfy $C(x) \subseteq Q(x)$, and they can differ; both facts are theorems on
    91	  this page, and the difference is exactly the gap between "cannot be split by a
    92	  clopen set" and "is connected".
    93	
    94	- **Quasicomponents are what a separation argument actually produces.** A proof
    95	  that two points cannot be separated typically produces a clopen set containing
    96	  both or neither, which is a statement about $Q$, not about $C$. Naming the
    97	  weaker notion keeps such an argument honest instead of letting it be read as a
    98	  connectedness claim.
    99	
   100	- **The definition of totally disconnected is stated with components, not with
   101	  quasicomponents.** The condition "every quasicomponent is a singleton" is a
   102	  different and strictly stronger property, usually called total separatedness.
   103	  Nothing on this page asserts that the two agree.

